## Context

`kotoba-lang/industry`'s registry carried a `\"5911\"` entry at
`:maturity :spec` with `:name \"Motion picture, video and television
programme production a...\"` (truncated -- one of the ~10%-of-entries
truncated-name seed-data artifacts this fleet has documented elsewhere;
matched the assigned ISIC class exactly at the truncation point,
confirming no ID/name mismatch, and de-truncated to the full ISIC Rev.4
name \"Motion picture, video and television programme production
activities\" as part of this same promotion) and `:repo
\"https://github.com/gftdcojp/cloud-itonami-J5911\"` (an old,
never-populated naming scheme). Confirmed no repo exists yet at either
that stale placeholder or the real
`cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isic-5911` target (`gh api` 404 for the
latter) before any work began. Sibling classes 5912 (post-production),
5913 (distribution), and 5914 (projection) are distinct, separately
built entries in this same batch; 5912's `:name` was confirmed to start
\"...post-product...\", 5913's \"...distribution...\", 5914's the full,
untruncated \"Motion picture projection activities\" -- none conflated
with this class's pre-production/production scope.

This is part of Wave 4 (human-facing/personal-services fleet, ISIC
sections I/P/Q/R/S/T), running in parallel with the ongoing Wave 3
(production/robotics) rollout per ADR-2607152500. Film/TV production
touches on-set physical-safety and talent-welfare dimensions (stunt
work, minor performers, working conditions), so per Wave 4's
person-facing-service safety guardrail this actor's closed op allowlist
must never include an op that directly finalizes an on-set-safety-
authority decision (clearing a stunt as safe, overriding a
minor-performer work-hour limit) -- those are always either a hard
permanent block or an always-escalate op, never auto-commit-eligible.

## Decision

Scaffold `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isic-5911` as a film/TV-production
OPERATIONS COORDINATION actor (not an on-set-safety authority, not an
equipment-actuation controller), mirroring `cloud-itonami-isic-873`'s
verified module shape (`store`/`advisor`/`governor`/`phase`/`operation`/
`sim`, `deps.edn`/`blueprint.edn`/README/GOVERNANCE/CODE_OF_CONDUCT/
CONTRIBUTING/SECURITY, AGPL-3.0-or-later) with fresh, film/TV-production-
specific domain logic under the `filmprodops` namespace:

1. **`filmprodops.store`** -- `MemStore` (atom of EDN) behind a `Store`
   protocol; a `productions` directory keyed by `:production-id` STRING
   (a film/TV production under way), plus an append-only `ledger` and a
   `coordination-log` of committed records.
2. **`filmprodops.advisor`** (\"FilmProductionAdvisor\") -- deterministic
   mock advisor drafting exactly four kinds of proposal: shoot-day/
   scene/take record logging (`:log-production-record`), shoot-day/
   location/crew scheduling (`:schedule-production-operation`), on-set
   safety-concern flagging (`:flag-onset-safety-concern`), and footage/
   dailies post-production handoff coordination
   (`:coordinate-post-production-handoff`). Every proposal's `:effect`
   is always `:propose`; every output is censored downstream by the
   governor.
3. **`filmprodops.governor`** (\"FilmProductionGovernor\") -- three HARD
   checks, all permanent and un-overridable: (1) production-unverified
   -- the target production record must exist AND be independently
   `:registered?`/`:verified?` in the store before ANY proposal for it
   may commit or escalate; (2) effect-not-propose -- any `:effect`
   other than `:propose` is HARD-blocked; (3) scope-exclusion -- any
   proposal (regardless of op) whose op/summary/rationale/cites/value
   touches finalizing an on-set safety-clearance decision (stunt-
   clearance sign-off, hazard-clearance sign-off), overriding a
   minor-performer work-hour limit, directly actuating rigging/stunt/
   pyrotechnic equipment, or finalizing talent-compensation/insurance/
   legal/union matters is a HARD, PERMANENT block, unconditionally
   evaluated on every proposal; an op outside the closed four-op
   allowlist is folded into this same check. One ESCALATE (soft) gate:
   `:flag-onset-safety-concern` ALWAYS escalates to a human regardless
   of confidence, as does low confidence generally.
4. **`filmprodops.phase`** -- Phase 0->3 staged rollout;
   `:flag-onset-safety-concern` is permanently ABSENT from every
   phase's `:auto` set (structural fact, not a rollout milestone still
   to come) -- only `:log-production-record`/`:schedule-production-
   operation`/`:coordinate-post-production-handoff` may auto-commit at
   phase 3 when governor-clean.
5. **`filmprodops.operation`** (\"OperationActor\") -- langgraph-clj
   StateGraph, `intake -> advise -> govern -> decide -> commit | hold |
   request-approval`, `interrupt-before #{:request-approval}` for
   human-in-the-loop sign-off, invoked exclusively via
   `langgraph.graph/run*`.
6. **`deps.edn` / `blueprint.edn` / docs** -- mirror
   `cloud-itonami-isic-873`'s shape (`:test`/`:lint`/`:run`/`:dev`
   aliases pinned to the same `kotoba-lang/langgraph` git SHA
   `a332a770a0d2b5193f81b54483bb954fb29ef8d7`, `itonami.blueprint/*`
   metadata, scope/design/testing README sections).

### Scope-exclusion phrasing discipline (fleet-wide self-tripping bug avoided proactively)

Multiple sibling agents in this batch independently discovered and
fixed the same bug class: a scope-exclusion term list phrased as a bare
noun (e.g. \"stunt\", \"minor\") accidentally matches inside the mock
advisor's own DEFAULT rationale/disclaimer text for a legitimate,
allowed proposal, causing the actor to self-block on its own happy
path. This build avoided that class from the start rather than
discovering it via a failing test: `filmprodops.governor/scope-excluded-
terms` are phrased exclusively as the finalization/execution ACTION
(\"finalize the stunt clearance\", \"override the minor performer
work-hour limit\"), never as the bare noun alone. A dedicated regression
test, `default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip` in
`test/filmprodops/governor_test.clj`, independently confirms every
allowed op's own default (clean) advisor-generated proposal text --
against every demo production -- clears the governor's scope-exclusion
scan.

### What this actor does NOT do

Finalizing an on-set safety-clearance decision (stunt-clearance
sign-off, hazard-clearance sign-off), overriding or finalizing a
minor-performer work-hour limit, directly actuating rigging/stunt/
pyrotechnic equipment, and finalizing talent-compensation/contract
terms or insurance/legal/union matters all remain exclusively
human/institutional decisions, permanently, with no actor or
human-approval override path -- enforced structurally by the
governor's closed op/effect allowlists and the unconditional
scope-exclusion check, not just documented. `:flag-onset-safety-concern`
is a \"surface the concern\" op only; it can never self-clear the concern
it raises, and is never a member of any phase's `:auto` set.

